Subject: The shell gives freed memory back to the system

plasmashell sets a small trim threshold in main(), which only returns what
is free at the top of the main heap. The holes left inside it, and inside
the arenas of the render threads, stay counted as the shell's memory: on
an idle desktop drawn in software, 30 MB of its 208 MB (measured on
2026-11-19, a fresh sign-in, three minutes idle).

A small timer now calls malloc_trim(0) once the desktop has settled (60 s
after start, so after Start has warmed up) and every 10 minutes after
that, for what popups and menus leave behind. A pass takes about 2.5 ms
the first time and under 0.5 ms later; pages come back only if used.

Reason: the holes are inside plasmashell's own heap, and only a call from
inside the process can return them. No glibc tunable does it (the trim
threshold only shrinks the top of the heap, as the test shows), and a
preloaded library is out because the shell must not depend on LD_PRELOAD.

